# SH-10 — the idol-native owner of SYMBOL emission.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Three separate canonical repairs — gap[083] name
# mangling, gap[087] bare-identity resolution, gap[088] constructor rung (a) —
# all terminate at ONE missing capability: there is no idol-native owner of
# code emission. `lib/compiler/` has a lexer, a parser and a projection.
# Nothing here owns a symbol.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. Not a code generator. It emits no declarations, no bodies, no
# records. It answers exactly one question — what symbol does this idol relation
# take — and that is the question gap[083] is about.
#
# THE DEFECT IT CLOSES, measured 2026-08-08: a idol function named `read`,
# `write`, `open` or `abs` emitted its name UNMANGLED, where libc had already
# declared it. `abs(21)` compiled clean and printed **21** — libc's answer, not
# idol's. Not a build failure. A wrong number.
#
# WHY UNCONDITIONAL RATHER THAN A KEYWORD LIST. libc's namespace is OPEN: any
# symbol it declares next release can capture a idol name, so the colliding set
# is unknowable from inside the compiler. A list is a census that goes stale and
# reports a confident zero. Mangling every relation needs no list and cannot go
# stale.
#
# ============================================================================
# THIS FILE IMPLEMENTED A RETIRED SCHEME, AND IT BUILT THE WHOLE TIME
# ============================================================================
# Measured 2026-08-16 by reading it: `root("f")` answered `duo_fn_f`,
# `module("compiler","lexer")` answered `compiler__lexer`, `entry()` answered
# `duo_entry_main`. That is `codegen.zig`'s C-path scheme, and `codegen.zig` is
# the RETIRED emitter.
#
# The host now answers `_idol_<home>__<name>`, and `main` bare. So the
# self-hosted compiler, on the day it emitted anything, would have produced
# symbols the host cannot meet — every cross-module call an `undefined symbol`
# at link.
#
# IT NEVER APPEARED AS A BLOCKED ROW. `gate/selfhost.sh` reads 8 of 18 and this
# file is one of the 8: it BUILDS. A row that is green and wrong is worth more
# attention than a row that is red, because nothing is going to trip over it.
#
# THE LAW, from `home_resolve.relationSymbol` — the host's single authority:
#
#   foreign declared  ->  that name        the declaration IS the name
#   name == "main"    ->  "main"           belongs to the C runtime
#   no home           ->  name
#   otherwise         ->  "idol_" + home + "__" + name, over the symbol alphabet
#
# `docs/cross-home-identity.md` is the ruling. The two exemptions are the whole
# list; "the program has one file" is deliberately NOT one, because a law with a
# special case for small programs stops being true exactly when a second file
# arrives.

# The symbol alphabet. Every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]` becomes `_`, which is
# what `home_resolve.appendSymbolBytes` does and why it exists: a home is a
# DOTTED path (`compiler.lexer`) and a file name may hold anything the file
# system allows. A hyphenated file emitted `.globl _idol_odd-name__f` once, and
# the assembler refuses that — the law is stated over the alphabet, not over the
# spelling.
safe: str = (text: str)
  out = ""
  i = 1
  n = text:len()
  while i <= n
    b = text:byte(i)
    keep = (b >= 97 and b <= 122) or (b >= 65 and b <= 90) or (b >= 48 and b <= 57) or b == 95
    if keep
      out = "{out}{text:sub(i, i)}"
    else
      out = "{out}_"
    i += 1
  out

# `main` is the one relation that may NOT take the home prefix: the process
# entry's name belongs to the C runtime, and a collision there is a link error
# rather than a silent bind. It is also the collision the law deliberately
# KEEPS — two programs cannot share a process entry.
entry: str = ()
  "main"

# Whether a relation takes the home prefix at all. Everything does except the
# entry — that IS the rule — and this exists so a caller states the decision
# rather than assuming it, and so the exception has somewhere to live.
mangles: bool = (name: str)
  if name == "main"
    return false
  true

# THE LAW ITSELF, and it is a function of home AND name. The host's own test
# says so in those words (`home_resolve: the symbol is a function of home AND
# name`), and the previous shape here — a `root(name)` taking only a name — is
# what let a scheme with no home in it look complete.
module: str = (home: str, name: str)
  "idol_{safe(home)}__{safe(name)}"

# The symbol for a relation, with both exemptions applied. A foreign boundary
# declares its own name and is not routed through here at all; that arm lives in
# the host beside this one, because two copies of an exemption list is how a
# definer and a caller stop agreeing.
symbol: str = (home: str, name: str)
  if mangles(name)
    return module(home, name)
  entry()
